While user trust and the adoption of AI had become an emerging research topic in earlier studies prior to 2015, they tended to focus on the effects of technological factors, e.g. accuracy (Stumpf et al., 2007), security (Liao et al., 2011), explainability (Pieters, 2011), creativity (Skulimowski, 2013), collaboratively (Li et al., 2014), design (Luxton, 2014). Despite the increase in empirical research attempts to understand the adoption of AI and its relation to trust and organizational practices, systematic reviews of the existing studies on this topic from various perspectives are still lacking. The growing popularity of AI applications makes a systematic review of the related literature critical for summarizing what has been done in this area and, at the same time, establishing a solid foundation for future research in the domain. Therefore, the present study is aimed at addressing this gap with the formulation of the following research questions:
• What are the components of trust in relation to AI? • What are the critical factors that influence users’ trust in
AI? • What are the outcomes of users’ trust in AI? • What is an appropriate conceptual model for better
understanding users’ trust in the adoption of AI?
To address these research questions, a systematic review of the related literature on user trust in AI was conducted. A total of 131 papers published between January 2015 and January 2022 were synthesized to identify the commonly occurring themes. This enabled the identification of relevant factors related to user trust that are affecting the adoption of AI in practice. Subsequently, an integrated framework was developed to better tackle these issues, which can further be tested and validated to enhance the knowledge of user trust and the adoption of AI.
This paper is divided into seven sections. "Research method" of this paper presents the methodology used for this study. "Overview of AI and user trust" presents an overview of the development of AI and user trust. "Literature review on trust in AI" provides a review of related literature. "Relevant theories on new technology adoption" provides the discussions and a comprehensive conceptual framework that illustrates the components, influencing factors, and outcomes of user trust in AI. "The components of trust in relation to AI" presents the limitations and future research. "Predictability, dependability, faith" concludes the paper.
Research method
The aim of this research study was to explore the issues related to user trust in AI based on the literature from the past years and thereby guide future research in this area. To this end,

Wolfswinkel et al.’s (2013) five-phase approach was adopted for conducting a systematic review and analysis of the literature. This approach enables researchers to conduct a thorough search process and critically review and analyze the articles retrieved from various databases. The five-phase approach includes (a) defining the scope of the review, (b) searching the literature, (c) selecting the final samples, (d) analyzing the samples using content analysis, and (e) presenting the findings.
Accordingly, the first phase of the present study was to define the scope of the review, which included defining the criteria for the inclusion and exclusion of relevant sources and the criteria for identifying and retrieving those sources in the literature. Several databases, including Web of Science, ScienceDirect, and Emerald, were used due to their reputation, representativeness, and coverage in publishing top academic papers in relevant fields (Zhu & Liu, 2020). For a broad coverage of the studies obtained from these databases, the following keywords were used for the search: artificial intelligence, AI, customer, user, and trust. The criteria for the inclusion and exclusion of articles were based on our research objectives. We included articles published from January 2015 to January 2022 because the volume of AI-related applications and research in different fields has dramatically increased, especially after 2015 (Baum et al., 2021; Fatima et al., 2021; Paek & Kim, 2021; Pena et al., 2021), and the search in databases was conducted in early February 2022.
The second phase involved in running the search query within the selected databases and retrieving the search results. A total of 2559 articles were returned using the above pre-defined search strings. Then, 810 duplicated articles were removed, and a total of 1749 articles remained for further assessment. This initial search enabled us to gain a general understanding of the coverage of user trust in AI-related research.
The third phase required selecting the final samples for a detailed analysis. We applied the following criteria for the inclusion and exclusion of articles based on our research objectives, after the initial screening. First, we only included fulltext articles published in academic journals and peer-reviewed conference proceedings related to our research objectives. Therefore, 895 publications (e.g. abstract-only or research-inprogress papers) were excluded. Second, studies presented in languages other than English were not considered to minimize regional over-representation of research in the formal analysis (Dreyer et al., 2019). Third, we excluded articles without a strong relevance to user trust, AI and its applications. Thus, the titles and abstracts of all the initial articles were screened for their relevance to our research topic. Accordingly, another 723 papers were removed. Then, we conducted a backward and a forward snowballing research recommended by Webster and Watson (2002). Accordingly, we adopted Google Scholar to find relevant publications from the references of the identified articles, resulting in 11 additional articles. After the above process, a total of 142 articles remained for further analysis.

