Overview of AI and user trust
AI has increasingly gained attention due to its capabilities in supporting common applications in everyday life, such as chatbots, facial recognition, image recognition, personalized recommendations, and other services (Wei et al., 2019; Hsiao & Chen, 2021). Even though AI positively impacts how individuals and organizations interact, there is a growing focus on its trustworthiness. Hasan et al. (2021) believe that trust plays a key role in helping individuals accept and use new technologies. For example, trust can alleviate users’ concerns about purchase transactions and product quality in electronic markets. Similarly, Yang & Wibowo (2020) find that trust plays an important role in users’ acceptance of e-government. These studies demonstrate that trust is an important factor in helping users overcome their doubts and fears, resulting in the adoption of new technologies such as AI.
Several studies (Oliveira et al., 2017; Nordheim et al., 2019; Wei et al., 2019; Gillath et al., 2021) have been conducted on user trust in the context of AI. Oliveira et al.

(2017) find that customers who trust the interaction with AI robots are likely to choose services provided by these robots in the first place. Trust is especially crucial in contexts where traditional face-to-face businesses are conducted over virtual media, such as e-commerce and electronic and online banking (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017). Nordheim et al. (2019) state that trust plays an important role in users’ acceptance and use of AI-enabled systems. This is because trust reduces the uncertainties and perceived risks in adopting new technologies. Wei et al. (2019) argue that trust can influence users’ behavioral intentions in using AI-based systems. Their study shows that the greater the trust customers have in the system that the organization offers, the higher their positive mindset towards using the organization’s services. Gillath et al. (2021) examine how the human-to-human attachment process can be applied to increase users’ trust in AI. They reveal that reducing anxiety and enhancing security associated with AI helps to increase trust in AI. Therefore, it is important to look into the issues and challenges related to users’ trust in the adoption of AI in organizations.
Literature review on trust in AI
Relevant theories on new technology adoption
Previous studies have shown that trust plays a vital role in increasing the adoption and diffusion of AI applications in different areas (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017; Gillath et al., 2021). There were various theoretical models developed by earlier studies on individuals’ adoption of new technologies, including the Theory of Reasoned Action (TRA) (Fishbein & Ajzen, 1977), Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) (Ajzen, 1991), Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (Davis, 1985), Technology–Organization–Environment framework (TOE) (Tornatzky et al., 1990), Diffusion of Innovation (DOI) (Rogers, 2003), Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) (Venkatesh

