In the recent years, the Corporate Digital Responsibility (CDR) framework has become an emerging focus in this field, which is “defined as the set of shared values and norms guiding an organization’s operations with respect to the creation and operation of digital technology and data.” (Lobschat et al., 2021, p. 876). This framework illustrates the CDR-building process and components, the influences on CDR, and the outcomes of CDR. Akin to the CSR theory, CDR emphasizes the values and norms in terms of organizations’ responsibility for their stakeholders (e.g. investors, employees, customers, and suppliers) and the significant role of corporate decision-makers (Elliott et a., 2021; Liyanaarachchi et al., 2020; Lobschat et al., 2021; Weber-Lewerenz, 2021). However, CDR has distinctiveness in terms of its primary focus on the digital aspect due to the “exponential growth in technological development, malleability of technologies and data in use, and pervasiveness of technology and data” (Lobschat et al., 2021, p. 876). This suggests that businesses can facilitate customers’ trust in AI by improving digital responsibility, such as ethical data management (Jelovac et al., 2021). While the CDR framework covers different stakeholder groups, there is still a need for more studies focusing on specific aspects (e.g. users) to complement it with a more in-depth understanding, such as behavior change models (Ryan, 2020; Lobschat et al., 2021). Table 2 provides an overview of the fundamental theories and their key constructs.
The components of trust in relation to AI
The concept of trust can be understood in general by studying an individual’s overall trusting beliefs in other people and things. However, a person’s specific trusting beliefs can

be inconsistent in different circumstances (Thiebes et al., 2021). Trust has previously been studied as an important factor that affects people’s acceptance of new technologies (Ashfaq et al., 2020; Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021; Mou et al., 2017; Moussawi et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021). Notably, trust was often a general concept in those studies (Moussawi et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021). However, as a fundamental concept, trust has been widely researched in various social science disciplines such as sociology, social psychology, philosophy, management, and economics (Rheu et al., 2021). Thus, the components of trust in relation to AI should be studied from different perspectives.
Predictability, dependability, faith
Trust is a dynamic concept that involves multiple stages (Rheu et al., 2021). According to one of the most popular approaches to defining trust in the social sciences, this concept includes three major stages such as predictability, dependability, and faith (Rheu et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019). Rheu et al. (2021) consider predictability as the consistency of a trustee’s behaviors or performance in the long term. When an AI application’s performance is consistent over an extended period of time and across various circumstances, it supports a more precise prediction of the application’s action with a certain level of confidence (Nordheim et al., 2019). Therefore, predictability is related to understandability, where users will be able to predict an AI’s future actions if they can understand how it works. This will encourage users to form a prevalent belief that the new technology is dependable (i.e. dependability) (Sheridan, 2019). Thus, a higher level of predictability indicates a lower likelihood of unintended consequences that are unanticipated and may pose negative impacts on society (Pringle et al., 2016). Faith is usually considered the final stage of the formation of trust. Only when an AI application’s performance is believed to be predictable and dependable will the user establish their faith and willingness to rely on the technology when needed (Rheu et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019).
Ability, benevolence, and integrity
Another most well-cited approach used to define trust in interpersonal relationships is to assess this concept based on multiple criteria, which include users’ belief in AI’s ability, benevolence, and integrity (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Pickering, 2021; Rheu et al., 2021; Schmidt et al., 2020; Thiebes et al., 2021). Ability concerns the skill of an AI application in completing a task assigned by the user (Rheu et al., 2021; Thiebes et al., 2021), as well as whether the AI can correctly decide and perform the most important of multiple tasks (Sheridan, 2019). Several researchers, such as Gulati et al. (2019), Nundy et al.

