assessed further from two specific perspectives: (i) trust in the technology and (ii) trust in the organization (Łapińska et al., 2021; Thiebes et al., 2021). The former includes users’ trust in AI’s various technological compo​nents, including the device, infrastructure, data transmission process, and analytic functions, while the latter is institution-based and focuses on the provider of AI-enabled services (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017; Gillath et al., 2021; Söllner et al., 2016; Wei et al., 2019). The ground for grouping the concept of trust into two individual categories is that people’s trust in technology is different from their trust in a human, organization, or community (Schmidt et al., 2020). Compared to trust towards human beings, “users tend to have less trust towards AI by default where people are especially averse to algorithmic forecasters after seeing them perform, even when they see them outperform a human forecaster” (Schmidt et al., 2020, p.4). This approach allows for more precise measurements of the components of trust. For instance, the ability competent of trust in AI technology is based on the characteristics of the AI-enabled service and its provider (Mal et al., 2018; Nundy et al., 2019). Thus, these characteristics should include not only the AI technology’s reliability and quality (e.g. artifact design and safeness) but also the organization’s (i.e. service provider) capability, management, strategy, and culture (Li et al., 2021; Mal et al., 2018). Meanwhile, the benevolence component of users’ trust in the AI technology is reflected by its genuine communications with users and care for their needs. It also requires the organization’s care towards its stakeholders, such as its employees, partners, the environment, and society. Furthermore, the integrity component of users’ trust depends on whether the AI technology is able to keep promises and provide fair and transparent services (Crigger et al., 2022; Crockett et al., 2020; Felzmann et al., 2019; Qin et al., 2020; Ryan, 2022; Shin, 2021). It also requires both the AI-enabled service and its provider to follow the norms and regulations (Gillath et al., 2021; Janssen et al., 2020; Kuipers, 2018). Thus, some researchers use the term “digital trust” that highlight the organizational and technical aspects to suggest the importance of CDR for user trust building (Jelovac et al., 2021). Digital trust suggests users’ trust should be not only adopted as a universal concept but related to specific fields (e.g. digital technologies).
Further, Li et al. (2021) also related users’ trust in an organization to its stakeholders, namely through the aspects of trust in other related organizations and trust in people in the organization. The former concerns users’ attitudes towards other relevant organizations within the supply chain of the AI service provider, for example, based on the information technology integration with its supplier(s). The latter refers to the user’s perception of the behaviors of employees and leaders in the related organizations. In this context, scholars have highlighted the importance of an

organization’s AI promoters, who are responsible for introducing, implementing, and promoting the new technology.
Thus, users’ trust in AI depends on their general trusting beliefs, trust in the associated organization, and trust in the technology. Thiebes et al.’s (2021) study illustrated the relationships between three factors: (i) a user’s general trusting beliefs in other people and new technologies will affect their (ii) trust in an organization’s adopted AIs, which will finally determine their (iii) trust in specific people (such as AI promoters) and the AI applications adopted by the organization. In this regard, Söllner et al.’s (2016) study highlighted the unique importance of trust in the organization as it significantly affects users’ trust in the technology. Further, Mou et al. (2017) argued that trust in the organization, compared to trust in the technology, plays an even more significant role in users’ acceptance of services supported by new technologies.
Cognitive, affective, and behavioral trust
The above discussions suggest those studies on users’ trust tends to focus on the trusted objects (e.g. technologies and organizations). However, the formation of trust should also depend on the components related to users. For instance, trust in technology and the organization originates from the qualitative characteristics (e.g. competence, ability, and integrity) perceived by the users (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019; Kraus et al., 2021; Shi et al., 2021). Thus, some researchers have classified this as the cognitive aspect of trust as it is generally based on the user’s rational cognition and calculation of losses and gains of trusting the technology (Gillath et al., 2021; Glikson & Woolley, 2020; Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019; Kraus et al., 2021). The formation of cognitive trust is often related to the reputation of the service provider organization in terms of the characteristics believed or communicated by other users. This aspect of trust is also role-based, as it originates from a consideration of the AI’s role in service (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019).
In addition to the cognitive aspect, scholars believe that user trust should also be studied from affective and behavioral perspectives. The affective (also known as emotive) aspect of trust focuses on users’ intellectual background and their feelings and attitudes towards AI. Compared to cognitive trust, the affective aspect is irrational due to its dispositional nature, which means that trust formation is significantly related to users’ psychological backgrounds, even if they are unfamiliar with AI (Gillath et al., 2021; Glikson & Woolley, 2020; Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019; Kraus et al., 2021; Shi et al., 2021). In this situation, users’ affective trust in AI is caused by their love or propensity to trust others, without identifying the technology or the service provider (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019). Therefore, affective trust can lead to users making decisions based only

