on their psychology while ignoring all the facts about AI and the related service providers (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019). Last, behavioral trust is based on repeated interactions between the user and AI applications over a period of time. Compared to affective trust, this type is based on a user’s direct and repeating experience with the identified service provider and technologies (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019). Thus, these two types of trust differ in terms of the basis of identification and relationship (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019; Li et al., 2021). Furthermore, behavioral trust is a form of direct trust, whereas cognitive trust may arise from other people’s recommendations. Therefore, the difference between behavioral and cognitive trust is the acquisition method involved (Li et al., 2021).
Trust in specific AI applications and contexts
The above discussions suggest that users’ trust in AI should be studied as a multiple-component concept. First, people may use similar criteria when forming trust in different objects. Therefore, users’ trust in AI should include the universal components (e.g. predictability, dependability and faith) that can also be applied to other trusted objects. Thus, these components reflect the generic requirements for trust building. Second, AI has been adopted by different organizations as new digital technology. Therefore, users’ trust in AI should also include the components of trust related to the technology and the organization. These components provide user trust, as a generic concept, with a focus on AI-supported service providers. Third, from the users’ perspective, they may form trust in AI based on their different knowledge, psychology, and interactions related to the technology and the organization. This accordingly suggests that the components of trust in AI should also include users’ cognitive, affective, and behavioral aspects. Further, it is noticeable that some scholars have limited the scope of users’ trust to specific AI applications. For instance, Park (2020) used the term “Trust in service robots” in his study on tourism management. Meanwhile, Qin et al. (2020) adopted the term “Trust in AI-based educational systems” for their research on elementary and secondary education. The adoption of AI technology in a wide range of sectors and contexts implies that a person’s trust in different AI applications may vary. This can be explained by the fact that users may have different levels of involvement with AI applications in various contexts (Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021). For example, even though a user accepts chatbots for customer services, he/she may not have the same level of trust toward self-driving cars, because the latter applies AI to a more concerning context. However, the above-mentioned three classes of user trust components have not considered the users’ inconsistent trust in various contexts of AI applications. For different applications, users may have to use different evaluation criteria

for AI’s performance, process, and purpose, have different expectations for the technology and the organization, and need different levels of knowledge and interactions. Based on this, we proposed an additional component of users’ trust in AI: the trust in specific applications. This component suggests that users may not necessarily expand their trust in one AI application to others. Therefore, even when a trusted organization applies the same technology to a new AI-supported product or service for the same users, it still needs to adjust the trust-building tactics.
Critical factors influencing trust in AI
The existing studies based on the UTAUT model suggest that users’ trust in AI is dependent on performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influence (Fan et al., 2020). Furthermore, trust is a synthesis of multiple components that can be studied from different perspectives. This implies that the formation of trust is affected by various factors. From the organizational environment perspective, influencing factors include the internal (e.g. technologies and strategies adopted by an organization) and external (e.g. industry context, social and cultural influences, and customers/users) aspects (Everson, 2003). For example, Qin et al. (2020) and Kaplan et al. (2021) classified antecedents of users’ trust as technology-related, context-related, and user-related. Other studies also related users’ trust to organizational (Li et al., 2021) and social aspects (Kuipers, 2018). Meanwhile, the CDR framework suggests that the influences include organization factors, customer factors, and social factors (Elliott et al., 2021; Lobschat et al., 2021; Weber-Lewerenz, 2021). Thus, the present study divides the influencing factors of users’ trust in AI into five major groups, i.e. technological, organizational, contextual, social, and personal factors.
Technology‑related factors
Trust in technology is an essential component of user trust and the basis of people’s perceptions of AI techn​ologi​es and applications. For instance, studies based on the UTAUT model suggest that people’s trust in AI is driven by its perceived quality in terms of compatibility, ease of use, and performance (Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021). Thus, technology characteristics affect effort expectancy and influence users’ trust in AI (Fan et al., 2020). However, the quality of a compatible, user-friendly and high-performing AI application is reflected by multiple facets. Thus, some scholars, such as Nasirian et al. (2017), explored the antecedent of trust by considering users’ perceptions of the service quality, information quality, and system quality of AI applications. A few studies have also considered perceived designed quality as a technology-related factor (Kim & Peterson, 2017; Nilashi et al., 2015).

