First, AI technologies are often applied to service sectors (Balakrishnan & Dwivedi, 2021). Therefore, users’ perception of service quality is an important factor in building trust (Ameen et al., 2021; Ashfaq et al., 2020; Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Hsiao & Chen, 2021; Kim & Peterson, 2017; Nasirian et al., 2017; Oliveira et al., 2017; Wibowo et al., 2020). Users evaluate service quality by comparing their perception of the service received with their expectation of what that service should offer (Ameen et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019). A service provider’s effort for providing high-quality services is often seen by users as a sign of its capability to display integrity and trustworthiness (Kim & Peterson, 2017; Wibowo et al., 2020). Researchers consider service quality a technology-related factor because it requires the support of the more advanced AI heuristics and algorithms, such as automatic improvements through machine learning based on experience and data use (Sheridan, 2019). In the context of AI-enabled services, users often require convenient and safe performances and have additional expectations, such as more speedy responses, a better understanding of user preferences, and additional customer support, compared to traditional services (Ameen et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019). Moreover, AI technologies should not just focus on individuals’ pre-purchase activities if they are to provide high levels of service quality, but they also need to support the customers after their transactions (Nasirian et al., 2017; Nilashi et al., 2015). Balakrishnan and Dwivedi (2021) used the concept of cognitive absorption to reflect on the quality of AI-enabled services. Specifically, a high-quality service allows users to control their interactions, piques their curiosity, offers them enjoyment, immerses them in the tasks, and even makes them lose track of time. Further, to provide high service quality, AIs must also demonstrate their empathy and compassion for users, which improves emotional trust (Kerasidou, 2020; Lv et al., 2022). Some researchers, such as Ameen et al. (2021), also related the AI-enabled service quality to users’ perceived quality of the information system and its provided content.
Second, perceived information quality depends on the contents provided by an AI application to its users (Ashfaq et al., 2020; Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Nasirian et al., 2017). Thus, a user’s trust in the AI application depends on whether it is able to provide accurate, factual, current, and complete content that supports the user’s tasks (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017; Kim et al., 2021; Kim & Peterson, 2017; Nasirian et al., 2017; Nilashi et al., 2015; Yen & Chiang, 2021). This high-quality content should also be based on traceable and analytical data (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017). When users communicate with an AI, they usually expect the conversations to be informative and avoid too many irrelevant messages (Yen & Chiang, 2021). Moreover, AIenabled information systems should avoid inappropriate content, such as offensive language (Hong & Williams,

2019). It is also essential to avoid providing biased or discriminating information (Thiebes et al., 2021). For example, according to Hong and Williams (2019), some AI-enabled crime prevention applications suggest that black prisoners have a higher likelihood of future crimes than other prisoner groups with similar criminal records, despite the lack of evidence. This prejudice caused by the biased automation of AI can be considered a form of the “automating inequality” issues that have drawn the attention of researchers in the recent years (Eubanks, 2018; Mañero, 2020). These issues suggest that the increasing automation of AI can worsen the inequality for disadvantaged groups (Eubanks, 2018). Some researchers attribute this to the human bias because AI applications are fundamentally built on data generated by humans (i.e. data scientists, engineers and users) or collected via systems and algorithms designed by humans (Ntoutsi et al., 2020; Sanclemente, 2021). Meanwhile, Lobschat et al. (2021) argue that the biased projection may be unintendedly caused by using machine learning algorithms based on historical data alone. To reduce the bias, it is suggested to enhance the diversity of data sets and the inclusion of multiple groups in algorithm development (Ntoutsi et al., 2020; Sanclemente, 2021). Further, Al-powered organizations should be aware of the longer-term mutability of their digital assets during the decision-making and operational processes (Lobschat et al., 2021).
Third, the system quality of an information technology application can be evaluated in terms of the reliability, flexibility, accessibility, and timeliness of its functional features (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Lazanyi, 2018; Nassar et al., 2020). Thus, when an information system is easy to access and contains no errors (e.g. miscalculations, inaccuracies, misinterpretations, overestimations or underestimations), it indicates its trustworthiness (Kim & Peterson, 2017; Triberti et al., 2020). The perceived system quality often concerns security issues and whether the AI-supported system has an established structural assurance, e.g. site authentication and privacy protection policies and regulations (Anwar, 2020; Chatterjee & Sreenivasulu, 2021; Kim & Peterson, 2017; Nasirian et al., 2017; Nilashi et al., 2015; Qin et al., 2020). In other words, users will have more confidence in an information system’s trustworthiness if it has the mechanisms to protect their shared personal information (e.g. identity, location and device), and provides the assurance that their sensitive data will be accessed and viewed only by authorized people (Ahmadi & Tutschku, 2017; Kim & Peterson, 2017; Robinson, 2020). Thus, a high-quality system should aim to confidentially, integrally, and authentically transmit data, which still requires more effort in on-going improving the transparency, tractability, and predictability of its processes (Felzmann et al., 2019; Girsang et al., 2020; Hepworth et al., 2020; Nassar et al., 2020; Qin et al., 2020; Shin, 2021). For example, AI algorithm developers should also recognize

