their ethical responsibility at the post-implementation stage, which requires continuous engagement and monitoring in the long term (Lobschat et al., 2021).
Fourth, a number of studies highlight the importance of the perceived design quality of AI-supported information systems. This type of quality perception can be enhanced by using aesthetic features of interfaces (e.g. color schemes) that are pleasing and attractive to users (Ameen et al., 2021; Faisal et al., 2016; Hsiao & Chen, 2021; Nilashi et al., 2015; Qin et al., 2020). A visually appealing design reflects the service provider’s capability and professionalism, which can contribute to the formation of user trust (Følstad et al., 2018; Kim & Peterson, 2017). An AI-supported information system should be understandable and personalized, allow users to navigate the content easily, and have the capability to support multimedia in order for its design to be considered of high quality (Anwar, 2020; Moussawi et al., 2021; Nilashi et al., 2015; Qin et al., 2020; Shi et al., 2021). Some scholars, such as Faisal et al. (2016), emphasize the importance of a user-friendly design. For example, the content displayed in an AI-supported interface should be easy to read, especially for visually impaired users.
Notably, the importance of anthropomorphism or humanness has gained attention in a significant number of recent studies (Bernotat et al., 2017; Følstad et al., 2018; Gulati et al., 2019; Hsiao & Chen, 2021; Kaplan et al., 2021; Kim & Song, 2021; Lazanyi, 2018; Lee & Chen, 2022; Li et al., 2021; Lu et al., 2022; Mohanty, 2020; Moussawi et al., 2021; Pelau et al., 2021; Shi et al., 2021; Troshani et al., 2020; Yen & Chiang, 2021). This is because the anthropomorphism of AI is believed to foster users’ emotional trust (Gulati et al., 2019; Shi et al., 2021). Troshani et al. (2020) defined anthropomorphic characteristics in terms of the ability to have a presence, make human connections, show emotion, emulate human communication, and take the initiative to help. Researchers also relate it to human features such as body shapes, facial expressions and small talk (Bernotat et al., 2017; Paradeda et al., 2016). This concept is related to the social presence theory, which refers to the subjective experience of being present with a natural person and having access to their thoughts and emotions. Thus, the “sense of being with another” (i.e. human warmth, sensitivity, and sociability) depends on the design of interfaces in human-AI interactions (Yen & Chiang, 2021; Zhang et al., 2021). Further, an AI’s anthropomorphism level is supported by its intelligence, including its ability to innovate, understand the subtleties of human interactions, solve problems, and consistently achieve expected outcomes (Pelau et al., 2021; Troshani et al., 2020).
Organizational factors
As previously discussed, individuals’ trust in organizations (i.e. institution-based trust) is another essential component of the concept of user trust that complements their trust in

technologies (Chen & Wen, 2021; Wei et al., 2019). Therefore, it is important to study organization-related factors as antecedents of trust, and a number of studies have focused on the image and reputation of AI-enabled service providers (Burda & Teuteberg, 2014; Hamid et al., 2015; Kaplan et al., 2021; Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019; Oliveira et al., 2017; Qin et al., 2020; Xin et al., 2015). Organizational factors are related to social evaluation, which is defined as the degree of an organization’s compliance with societal norms and regulations (Hamid et al., 2015; Jelovac et al., 2021; Lobschat et al., 2021; Ryan, 2020). Thus, an organization’s reputation can be represented by its social and digital responsibility, e.g. having environmentally friendly policies, supporting local communities, treating employees fairly, and ethical data use (Elliott et al., 2021; Jelovac et al., 2021; Liyanaarachchi et al., 2020; Lobschat et al., 2021; Nguyen & Pervan, 2020; Weber-Lewerenz, 2021). The effect of organizational reputation on users’ trust is especially significant in the case of e-commerce (Oliveira et al., 2017). This may be because an organization’s reputation reflects its time and effort to offer sincere customer care. E-commerce service providers are usually recognized by their brand identities (organization names, trademarks, and symbols). Users in the e-commerce context may consider brand identities as substitutes for the information of a purchased service during the decision-making process. Therefore, a reputable brand tends to gain more consumer trust (Söllner et al., 2016).
The reputation of a service provider is often shared among users and, therefore, plays a significant role in fostering trust; this also extends to AI (Nordheim et al., 2019). To build user trust, organizations should demonstrate integrity, especially in data protection against misuse, which has become a public concern for AI technologies (Pickering, 2021; Weitz et al., 2019). Therefore, organizations need to develop and maintain relevant policies, principles, and procedures, including ethical committees, risk assessments, and regular audits (Janssen et al., 2020). A trustworthy service provider also needs to have a healthy organizational culture, ensuring the adherence to professional norms, perception of data as an asset, and implementation of rewards and sanctions (Jain et al., 2020; Wibowo & Mubarak, 2020). A user’s trust in a service provider is also based on the latter’s expertise and knowledge of its users, and timely and regular communication with customers (Alhazmi, 2019). Furthermore, reputation is often based on third-party assessment and recognition. In other words, people’s decisions are usually dependent on others’ opinions regarding the trustworthiness of the object. Therefore, gaining testimonials from a recognized expert or authority in the field can help an AI-enabled organization to be trusted and accepted (Triberti et al., 2020).
Moreover, AI technologies can not only support an organization’s customer service (e.g. chatbots) but may be

