applied to other management functions, such as production and manufacturing. Thus, the employees of the organization would also be users of AI technologies in this scenario (Łapińska et al., 2021; Li et al., 2021; Ryan, 2020). Accordingly, some researchers have focused on the trustworthiness of AI technologies within organizations. For instance, the commitment of an organization’s leaders (i.e. management commitment) to the implantation of AI in the organization is a critical factor. Leaders with a strong will to adopt AI in the organization are more likely to have solid strategic visions, invest greater resources, and set more incentivizing rules (Li et al., 2021). Furthermore, employees’ trust in AI is also related to the organizational culture. For instance, Li et al. (2021) argued that authoritarian culture can support trust in AI within the organizations because the autocratic leadership involved may motivate subordinates to trust in AI applications to maintain the consistency of ideas with their leaders.
Considering the above, the organizational approach explores the role of AI-enabled service providers’ characteristics in user trust (Chen & Wen, 2021). From the CDR perspective, studies in this field have stressed the significance of the lawful and ethical use of AI technologies (Bejger & Elster, 2020; Lobschat et al., 2021). First, the adoption of AI requires the assurance of fairness and honesty and the avoidance of discrimination (e.g. sexism or racism) against any user group (Bejger & Elster, 2020; Crigger et al., 2022; Hong & Williams, 2019; Jain et al., 2020; Qin et al., 2020; Sheridan, 2019; Shin, 2021; Thiebes et al., 2021; Wirtz et al., 2019). Therefore, AI-enabled organizations must consider the diversity of user groups and their abilities, skills, and needs to remain ethical and trustable (Bejger & Elster, 2020; Hong & Williams, 2019). Second, the use of AI technologies should respect human autonomy (Crigger et al., 2022; Jain et al., 2020; Kraus et al., 2021; Qin et al., 2020; Ryan, 2020). Consumers may be concerned about the virtual relationship between AI applications and users, and expect humans to be in the decision loop. Thus, organizations should allow users to make their own choices and have complete control over their data (Bejger & Elster, 2020; Sheridan, 2019). Third, AI-enabled organizations should use the relevant technologies to further their social responsibility and support environmental sustainability (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Bejger & Elster, 2020; Lobschat et al., 2021; Ryan, 2020). Therefore, organizations should consider the beneficence of using AI technologies, which requires balanced costs and benefits for their stakeholders. For example, organizations should allow users to recognize how their data are collected, used and stored, and provide them with valuable returns, e.g. monetary compensation and improved services (Lobschat et al., 2021). Ensuring the lawful and ethical use of AI technologies in line with the above does not only require secure, reliable, and robust AI algorithms but accountable culture and transparent governance

and culture of the organization (Bejger & Elster, 2020; Crigger et al., 2022; Felzmann et al., 2019; Lobschat et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019; Sutrop, 2019; Winfield & Jirotka, 2018). For example, to protect users’ data, it expects AI-enabled organizations to adopt encryption technologies, even though more investments are needed. It also requires the established CDR culture, in which AI application developers are responsible for consequences that arise from their duties (Lobschat et al., 2021).
Context‑related factors
The role of organizational factors may vary because organizations apply AI technologies in different contexts (e.g. industry context) (Araujo et al., 2020; Kolasinska et al., 2019; Lobschat et al., 2021; Wirtz et al., 2019). This can also be attributed to the fact that people may use different AI-enabled applications for various purposes and tasks (Ahn et al., 2022). The task complexity affects performance expectancy, influencing users’ trust in AI (Fan et al., 2020). Thus, it is also necessary to consider the effects of various features of AI-enabled services/products on users’ trust in different contexts. Many studies in this field apply the UTAUT/UTAUT2 to different contexts. This theory suggests that people’s acceptance of new technology will depend on their perception of its utilitarian features (e.g. perceived usefulness and ease of use) and hedonic motivation (perceived enjoyment) (Ashfaq et al., 2020; Moussawi et al., 2021). Bilgihan (2016), too, argued that the utilitarian and hedonic elements of a product (or service) will affect users’ trust in it.
The utilitarian aspect concerns how an AI-supported application can offer value to users’ task-related and rational consumption (Bilgihan, 2016). Existing studies based on UTAUT models highlight certain utilitarian features (i.e. perceived usefulness and ease of use) as the driving factors of user trust in different contexts (Zhang et al., 2021). However, users’ perceptions of the utilitarian value can differ across the various contexts of AI usage. For instance, explainability, which is the ability to interpret and justify the causal relationships between different factors, is essential for news recommendation systems or medical computer systems (Crigger et al., 2022; Li et al., 2021). User trust in chatbots will depend on the AI applications’ expertise, which is reflected by their knowledge of service context and user needs (Nordheim et al., 2019; Yen & Chiang, 2021). The utilitarian values of AI are also based on the error costs or the perceived severity in different contexts (Saßmannshausen et al., 2021; Sheridan, 2019; Vassilikopoulou et al., 2018). A higher level of perceived severity will make it more challenging to develop user trust (Vassilikopoulou et al., 2018). For example, it is typically considered a severe situation when people apply AI technologies to vehicles or medical expert systems because of the direct effects on their

