safety and health (Gupta et al., 2021; Pitardi & Marriott, 2021; Vourgidis et al., 2018).
The hedonic aspect is based on users’ subjective experiences of fun and playfulness (Bilgihan, 2016). For example, consumers may perceive the hedonic value of AI in terms of how it entertains them while providing a service (Lou & Yuan, 2019). The hedonic experience can also be related to the novelty of AI technologies. For instance, a study by Hasan et al. (2021) on voice-controlled AI applications revealed that users perceive a novelty value when the technology provides unique experiences that satisfy their curiosity. Users may demand different hedonic values of AI technologies in different contexts. For example, Li et al.’s (2021) study revealed that the consistency and personality of voice are important for in-vehicle assistants and smart speakers. Further, anthropomorphic characters (e.g. with the capability to interact in a natural language), social interactions (Shin, 2021), and playfulness are believed to be important factors for building users’ trust in chatbots (Nordheim et al., 2019; Yen & Chiang, 2021). Emotional factors (e.g. anxiety) affect users’ trust in self-driving vehicles and medical diagnostic aids (Li et al., 2021). Human-like representation in terms of appropriate emotional expressions and matching users’ personalities may also be required for AI applications in service contexts, such as social robots (Kerasidou, 2020; Langer et al., 2019). In the case of AI-supported appliances, users may consider aesthetic appearances a hedonic value (Sheridan, 2019).
In practice, integrating both utilitarian and hedonic aspects can be more efficient in building user trust. Triberti et al. (2020) highlighted exploitability as an important utilitarian feature, i.e. interpreting the processes and outcomes in intelligible ways for users who are supposed to benefit from the AI’s support. They also emphasized the necessity to develop the hedonic features of AI devices and interfaces to ensure that they are entertaining, engaging, mildly challenging and allow personalization.
Social factors
People, organizations and industries are components of the social environment. Thus, social factors such as social norms and culture are traditionally considered in the domain of human interactions. With the trend of AI becoming more intelligent, people’s trust in AI will increasingly be akin to that of trusting another human. Hence, the roles of social norms and culture will also be more significant in trusting an AI (Crigger et al., 2022; Fan et al., 2020; Fang et al., 2022; Kuipers, 2018; Sheridan, 2019). Some AI applications (e.g. chatbots) have been seen as social actors with the capacity to possess human-like attitudes or feelings. Therefore, users are likely to apply some social rules, such as manners, when interacting with AIs. Furthermore, the concept of user trust

includes the component of integrity, which requires both an AI-enabled service and its provider to follow societal norms. Thus, several studies have explored the social factors related to building user trust in AI (Gillath et al., 2021; Janssen et al., 2020; Kuipers, 2018; Sheridan, 2019). For example, trust in an AI application (e.g. robots) would depend on its compliance with social norms and morals, e.g. care, fairness, loyalty, and purity/sanctity (Alaieri & Vellino, 2016; Banks, 2020; Sullivan et al., 2020). Notably, a higher requirement for purity may reduce the level of religious groups’ trust in AI applications because the technologies may be considered intrinsically impure due to being artificial constructs that lack human souls and hearts (Banks, 2020). The effect of social rules may depend on whether they have become clear laws and regulations. For example, people are more likely to trust AI when users’ consent is a well-enforced legal requirement for data collection (Jelovac et al., 2021; Lobschat et al., 2021).
As another important social factor, culture can directly influence people’s perceptions, behaviors, and preferences in any nation (Robinson, 2020). Therefore, a stream of research focuses on relating social factors to cultural variance in different countries or markets. They aim to determine how the features of national cultures (i.e. individualism/collectivism, uncertainty avoidance, long-term/short-term orientation, power distance, and masculinity/femininity) affect people’s disposition to trust AI (Faisal et al., 2016; Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Robinson, 2020). First, as one of the cultural dimensions, individualism and collectivism describe the relationships of individuals within a nation or cultural group. In individualist cultural groups, people tend to focus on themselves and their immediate family, while collectivism values the strong ties between individuals in society. Thus, people from collectivist groups are more likely to trust others, especially their ability (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Robinson, 2020). Second, uncertainty avoidance is the extent to which individuals perceive and react to threats due to unknown situations and ambiguity (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Robinson, 2020). People often experience uncertainties when adopting a new technology because the outcome is unknown. Therefore, uncertainty avoidance reduces users’ disposition to trust (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Robinson, 2020; Schmidt et al., 2020). People from a country with a higher level of uncertainty avoidance will attach more importance to the security and simplicity of new technology and its applications (Faisal et al., 2016). Third, long-term oriented groups are likely to focus on the future, whereas short-term oriented groups emphasize the importance of the present and past (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Robinson, 2020). Thus, long-term oriented people tend to value the long-term trust that is built on not only the ability but also the consistency of benevolence and integrity of the trustee (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018).

