Fourth, power distance refers to people’s values regarding status, authority, and power in organizations and society. A culture group with high power distance tends to expect the leaders to have the authority and ability to make the right decisions regarding the adoption and implementation of AI technologies (Lei & Rau, 2021). Power distance also increases the ethical expectations placed on leaders’ decisions. Accordingly, it affects the benevolence and integrity components of user trust. Last, a masculine culture is actionoriented and focuses on achieving goals, while a feminine culture attaches more importance to harmonious relationships among the people. It, therefore, implies that feminine cultural groups tend to believe that “most people can be trusted” (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018).
User‑related factors
As discussed in the previous section, studies on social factors (e.g. national cultures) explore how users’ trust in AI is affected by society at the macro level. However, users’ trust is also affected by micro-level factors, i.e. their personal backgrounds and characteristics (Saßmannshausen et al., 2021; Starke et al., 2021). Scholars often relate user trust to the timing of the occurrence of trust (Li et al., 2021). For instance, Qin et al. (2020) and Sheridan (2019) argued that users’ familiarity and prior experiences with the technology can affect their tendency to trust. It implies that positive past experiences reduce the user’s difficulty in trusting an AI technology, while negative prior experiences (such as privacy issues) make the process more difficult (Lobschat et al., 2021). This viewpoint is consistent with the findings of studies by Bawack et al. (2021), Gillath et al. (2021), Khaksari and Keyvanpour (2019) and Söllner et al. (2016). Further, Sutrop (2019) stated that prior experiences are a type of empirical knowledge; it means people gain knowledge from their experience with an object. In the same vein, Kaplan et al. (2021) and Saßmannshausen et al. (2021) classified past experience as an element of ability-based antecedents of trust, which is also related to users’ knowledge, competency, expectancy, and expertise (i.e. expert status). Trust is required when a user has imperfect knowledge concerning a new technology (Starke et al., 2021). Thus, trust is unnecessary for users with complete knowledge about new technology, whereas those without knowledge, competency, or expertise cannot reasonably and thoroughly build trust. Those without any direct prior experience of using AI may rely on their recent experience with other new technologies, especially those offered by the same provider (Langer et al., 2019). Therefore, the users’ trust in AI can be built on direct or indirect experiences. Chong et al. (2022) and Hafızoğlu and Sen (2018) related the role of prior experiences in trust forming to its effect on users’ emotions, attitudes,

confidence, and expectations. For instance, previous negative experiences may lead to users being more stressed or cautious, making it more difficult for them to rely on AIs. A mismatch between a user’s expectation and the reality of AI applications can negatively affect trust (Wirtz et al., 2019). However, Hafızoğlu and Sen’s (2018) study suggests that prior experience with an AI-enabled service provider can only affect the initial trust. In other words, the effect of prior experience on trust diminishes over time.
User-related factors also include the characteristics of users’ demographic backgrounds and personality traits. The education level, socioeconomic status, age/generation, and other demographic characteristics likely affect users’ understanding, adoption, and use of new technologies (Araujo et al., 2020; Lou & Yuan, 2019; Sheridan, 2019). For instance, Kaplan et al. (2021) reported that several demographic factors (i.e. age, education, and gender) can affect users’ trust in AI, of which gender is the most influential one. The authors suggested that male users are more likely to trust AI than female users. This finding is consistent with a study by Dos Santos et al. (2019) on medical students’ attitudes toward AI. Some researchers argued that females are more likely to be anxious and risk-averse before accepting new technologies; therefore, females tend to have more security and privacy concerns when adopting new applications (Shao et al., 2020). As for the effect of age, it has been found that younger people are more likely to trust AI than older groups (Gillath et al., 2021). This is often attributed to the lower level of digital literacy and experience, and the higher likelihood of visual, physical and cognitive impairments among the older groups. It increases the difficulty of processing the information regarding new technologies and operating the new applications, which can accordingly reduce the trust level (Hua et al., 2021; Liyanaarachchi et al., 2020). Additionally, people’s educational level is positively associated with their trust in AI (Chen & Wen, 2021). This can be explained by the fact that higher education provides people with more knowledge of risk management (Jung, 2015), which may reduce their aversion to the risks of adopting new technologies. From the CDR perspective, AIpowered organizations should adopt a more user-centered approach to provide customized solutions (e.g. additional training and support services) to address vulnerable groups’ concerns and needs (Liyanaarachchi et al., 2020; WeberLewerenz, 2021).
Furthermore, a number of studies have underlined the significant role of users’ personalities in their trust in AI (Bawack et al., 2021; Gillath et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021; Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). These studies commonly adopted the Big Five model to assess the personality traits of AI users; this model measures the dimensions of openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism (Bawack

