et al., 2021; Gillath et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021; Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). Openness is the term for “openness to experience”, which can be defined as an individual’s propensity to be intellectually curious and open to ideas (Bawack et al., 2021; Li et al., 2021; Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). In other words, those with high openness value novelty, appreciate art, tend to adopt imaginative and creative thinking styles, and enjoy seeking deeper meaning in things. Conversely, a low level of openness indicates a preference for familiarity and traditions (Bawack et al., 2021; Gillath et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021; Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). Conscientiousness is represented by people’s ability to control, regulate, and direct their impulses in their decisions and behaviors. A higher level of this personality trait suggests a preference for being cautious and organized, whereas a lower level indicates a tendency toward being flexible and casual (Bawack et al., 2021; Gillath et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021). Extraversion (also spelled as extroversion) describes people who tend to be talkative and fast-paced. They prefer to draw the attention of others. Conversely, introverted individuals are unlikely to start conversations and feel uncomfortable around people (Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). Agreeableness can be reflected by the importance of cooperation and social harmony. In other words, agreeable people tend to be more obliged, kind, sympathetic, and accommodating. Thus, they value maintaining positive relationships with others. In contrast, individuals with lower levels of agreeableness are less likely to compromise their interests for others and tend to challenge others’ opinions (Gillath et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Oksanen et al., 2020). Neuroticism describes an individual’s emotional stability. People with a higher level of this personality trait tend to get stressed out and panic easily. By contrast, a lower level indicates the ability to control negative emotions such as anxiety, anger, and depression (Bawack et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Oksanen et al., 2020; Sharan & Romano, 2020). Researchers in this field tend to agree that all the five personality traits are predictors of trust in AI. Specifically, people are more likely to trust AI when they have high levels of openness, conscientiousness, agreeableness, extraversion and a low level of neuroticism (Bawack et al., 2021; Kaplan et al., 2021). However, the effect of an extroverted personality on user trust in the AI is inconsistent in the existing research, with some studies finding that extraversion contributes to a higher level of trust while others show the opposite effect (Kaplan et al., 2021).
Moreover, the propensity or disposition to trust is often studied as a type of personality that affects users’ trust in technologies (Agag & El-Masry, 2017; Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Kaplan et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Nordheim et al., 2019; Qin et al., 2020). This concept is defined as

the general tendency for an individual to trust others, which has a global effect on trust intentions (Agag & El-Masry, 2017; Fan et al., 2020; Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018; Kaplan et al., 2021; Langer et al., 2019; Qin et al., 2020; Wei et al., 2019). Stated differently, a higher level of propensity or disposition to trust indicates the greater tendency of an individual to trust other people. This tendency can also be applied to people’s trust in products and services. For example, Nordheim et al. (2019) used the term “propensity to trust technology” to study AI applications for customer services. People’s propensity to trust is affected by their personalities (Kim & Peterson, 2017; Langer et al., 2019) as well as their digital affinity, represented by the “degree to which the human has attraction, interest and pleasure in learning/using digital methods/technologies (Saßmannshausen et al., 2021, p. 1337). Similarly, Fan et al. (2020) adopted the concept of “personal innovativeness” to represent a person’s willingness to try out new technology. Personal innovativeness has a positive effect on effort expectancy and, accordingly, contributes to users’ initial trust (Fan et al., 2020).
Users’ trust in new technology is also driven by their existing perceptions. Risk is a necessary precondition that influences people’s trust in new technologies, including AI; therefore, the significant role of risk perception has been widely discussed in this field (Araujo et al., 2020; Crockett et al., 2020; Følstad et al., 2018; Gillath et al., 2021; Kaplan et al., 2021; Park, 2020; Starke et al., 2021; Sullivan et al., 2020; Sutrop, 2019; Yang et al., 2015). For example, people who perceive risks to their safety, privacy, or employment are less likely to trust AI (Wirtz et al., 2019). People may perceive various types of risks, including economic/financial, function/performance, physical/security, time/opportunity, privacy, psychological, and social (Gupta et al., 2021; Jiang & Lau, 2021; Sheridan, 2019; Triberti et al., 2020). First, economic risk refers to the possibility of financial loss due to using AI in relevant activities, such as investments or online transactions. Second, function risk is the potential that an AI-enabled service will not provide as valuable a performance as expected. Third, the physical risk is related to factors that can harm the user’s health or personal security. Fourth, time/opportunity risk is perceived when a user believes that their time and energy may be wasted by using an AI-enabled service or that they would miss out on the opportunity to use a better alternative. Fifth, privacy risk is the possibility of losing control over personal information. Sixth, social risk refers to the possibility of a user losing status in their social groups due to their failure to adopt AIenabled services. Last, the psychological risk is the possibility of the above-mentioned risks affecting the user’s mood or causing anxiety (Gupta et al., 2021; Jiang & Lau, 2021; Sheridan, 2019; Triberti et al., 2020). The research in this field agrees on the negative effect of risk perceptions on user trust (Araujo et al., 2020; Hampshire, 2017; Kaplan et al.,

