Outcomes of trust in AI
Cognitive and affective changes
Trust facilitates cognitive and affective changes in AI users. In other words, trust can affect users’ perceptions, opinions, beliefs, and emotions about using AI-enabled services from different providers (Vasiljeva et al., 2021). For instance, trust positively affects users’ subsequent perceptions regarding the benefits of using AI (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021). Moreover, users tend to believe using an AI-enabled system is more memorable, entertaining, exciting, comfortable, and educational after trust is built.
Attitude change is an expected outcome when a user’s trust in a service is gained or lost (Kim & Peterson, 2017; Vasiljeva et al., 2021). This concept can be defined in relation to the user’s disposition to respond favorably or unfavorably toward a service provider and its offers (Kim & Peterson, 2017). When AI-enabled service providers demonstrate their trustworthiness (i.e. ability, benevolence, integrity), the user’s attitude and feelings are more likely to be positive, resulting in a willingness to adopt (Lee & Chen, 2022; Vasiljeva et al., 2021). Furthermore, Ameen et al. (2021) reported a positive effect of user trust on subsequent experiences. This can, in turn, lead to user satisfaction, which refers to the contentment of a user in relation to a positive experience with a product or service (Kim & Peterson, 2017). A user’s trust in new technology is based on consistent and positive encounters; these also lead to a higher likelihood of satisfaction (Ashfaq et al., 2020; Hsiao & Chen, 2021; Kim & Peterson, 2017). The cognitive and affective changes may accordingly affect users’ behaviors (Ashfaq et al., 2020; Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021).

Behavioral changes
Another outcome of trust is the behavioral change exhibited by the user, and this is an important domain of research. For instance, the intention to adopt or use AI has become the primary topic for studies on the consequences of user trust (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Fan et al., 2020; Kim et al., 2021; Meyer-Waarden & Cloarec, 2022; Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021; Moussawi et al., 2021; Park, 2020; Priyadarshinee et al., 2017; Rahman et al., 2021; Starke et al., 2021; Vourgidis et al., 2018). The findings of these studies agree on the positive correlation between user trust and the usage of AI in various contexts. For example, Mostafa and Kasamani (2021) found that initial trust increases chatbot usage intention and user engagement in the context of online customer services. Chatterjee and Sreenivasulu (2021) reported that user trust has a positive effect on personal information-sharing behaviors on online platforms. Likewise, Park (2020) reported a positive and significant effect of trust on usage intention in the context of AI-enabled tourism services. Furthermore, users are more likely to reveal their personal and confidential information to a trusted AI (Ameen et al., 2021; Rahman et al., 2021).
However, Moussawi et al. (2021) reported a positive but insignificant effect of initial trust (i.e. ability, integrity, and benevolence) on people’s intention to adopt intelligent personal agents. Notably, this study and the ones mentioned above explored the role of initial trust at an early stage of AI usage (i.e. intention to adopt), and did not consider the continuing effects of trust on users’ long-term behavior. This can, however, be explained by Morgan and Hunt’s (1994) commitment-trust theory, which proposes a positive effect of trust on one’s relationship commitment to a product or service provider (Ameen et al., 2021). Further, Hsiao and Chen (2021), Lv et al. (2022), and Jiang and Lau (2021) suggested that user trust can affect continuance intention,

