especially when fewer physical and psychological risks are perceived. User trust does not only increase the initial intention to use relevant applications but contributes to consumers’ repurchase intentions and loyalty in the context of e-commerce (Bilgihan, 2016; Bitkina et al., 2020; Cheng & Jiang, 2021; Elbeltagi & Agag, 2016; Hasan et al., 2021; Kim & Peterson, 2017) and offline shopping (Ameen et al., 2021). Thus, a higher level of trust indicates that users are more committed to the service provider and less likely to switch to alternatives.
Discussions and research model
Every individual holds a general trust in other people and things along with varying trusting beliefs in different technologies. The extant literature is focused on the various aspects of users’ trust in AI, including its components, influencing factors, and outcomes. The components of users’ trust can be studied using different approaches. First, trust is dynamic and formed via different stages: The formation of user trust in AI starts when the user believes that the technology has shown consistent performance and is predictable. Further trust gets built on the user’s overall belief in the AI’s dependability in the long term. As the final stage of trust, the user has faith in the AI and will rely on the technology in the future when needed. Second, users may use different criteria to assess the trustworthiness of AI. The multiple-criteria approach suggests that trust is only formed when AI has the ability to competently perform the given tasks, present the benevolence to be aligned with users, and demonstrate the integrity to consistently follow moral principles. Some existing studies have synthesized the different stages and criteria of trust formation, namely trust in the performance, process, and purpose of AI. Furthermore, users may perceive AI as a technologysupported service or product that an organization provides. Thus, researchers have also investigated users’ trust in AI in terms of their trust in the technology and service provider organizations. Moreover, the components of user trust in AI can be distinguished in terms of how they are oriented, including the associated rational cognitions, affective feelings and attitudes, and behavioral interactions. Further, the concept users’ trust in AI should also consider its specific applications.
Considering the various components of user trust in AI, its influencing factors should be studied from different perspectives. The literature review findings suggest that user trust in AI is influenced by technological, organizational, contextual, social, and personal factors. First, technology-related factors concern the management of the service quality, information quality, system quality, and design quality of AI. Second, as discussed, user trust in an AI depends on components related to both the technology and the service provider organization. Thus, the organizational antecedents concern

the characteristics of AI-enabled service providers. Third, users’ perceptions and assessments of the performance of AI technologies are related to the contexts of use. Therefore, the level of user trust in an AI will depend on its utilitarian and hedonic value in different contexts. Fourth, social norms and national cultures influence people’s preferences, perceptions, and behaviors. Thus, they are often studied as social-related factors that affect users’ trust in AI. Last, users have different prior experiences and familiarity with the technologies, personalities, and existing perceptions related to AI. Thus, the formation of trust in AI is also influenced by user-related factors.
Regarding the outcomes, users’ trust affects cognitive and affective changes. Trust contributes to users’ positive perceptions, opinions, beliefs, and emotions about using AI-enabled services from different providers. It can also change users’ behaviors toward AI, including their intention to adopt and commitment. Based on the above discussion, Fig. 1 depicts a comprehensive conceptual framework that illustrates the components, influencing factors, and outcomes of user trust in AI.
Limitations and future research
The findings of the literature review show that the driving factors of user trust in AI can be categorized into five facets: technological, contextual, organizational, social, and user-related. It should be noted that the different facets of antecedents of user trust in AI are correlated and interact with each other. First, user perceptions of the technological qualities of AI vary in different groups. For instance, anthropomorphic designs are essential for older user groups (Rheu et al., 2021) and should also consider the personality match between the user and AI (Yen & Chiang, 2021). Such scenarios present interactions among technological and user-related factors.
Second, users’ perceptions of AI, such as perceived risks, can affect their trust and acceptance levels. However, the effects of such perceived risks are moderated by the severity of consequences of risky behaviors in different contexts (Starke et al., 2021; Vassilikopoulou et al., 2018). Thus, it suggests a relationship between user-related and context-related factors.
Third, the effect of an AI’s anthropomorphic facial expressions on user trust was found to be inconsistent in different experimental contexts (Rheu et al., 2021). The authors suggest that users tend to trust AI agents with male appearances when choosing utilitarian products, while they prefer recommendations from female AI agents in the context of hedonic products. Therefore, it is recommended that future studies compare the technological and user-related antecedents of user trust in AI across various contexts of use.
Fourth, a user’s propensity to trust is influenced by the national culture, such as collectivism and long-term

