orientation (Hallikainen & Laukkanen, 2018). These social antecedents of users’ trust in AI are also linked to the technological and organizational facets. For instance, users from uncertainty-avoiding culture groups will attach more importance to the security and simplicity of new technology and its applications (Faisal et al., 2016). Service providers should be compliant with the principles of economic and social justice (Alhazmi, 2019). Thus, societal expectations, norms, and values affect the policies, principles, and procedures (e.g. data governance) of organizations that adopt AI technologies (Janssen et al., 2020). For example, more rigorous data governance is required by organizations that provide AI-enabled services to groups that are sensitive to privacy issues (Chatterjee & Sreenivasulu, 2021; Janssen et al., 2020). Despite the interactions between the different influencing factors of user trust in AI, there remains a lack of studies that simultaneously include the various facets in their research design. This, therefore, calls for more comprehensive studies in this field.
A few researchers use the term initial trust in their studies on the user-AI interactions (Fan et al., 2020; Hafızoğlu & Sen, 2018; Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021; Moussawi et al., 2021). This implies that the findings of current studies in this field tend to be limited to the early stage of trust formation. This can be attributed to several reasons: First, many studies have focused on new users’ intentions to accept new AI technologies (Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021; Moussawi et al., 2021; Park, 2020; Shi et al., 2021). Second, empirical studies in this field tend to rely on data collected via cross-sectional surveys. Third, people’s trust in AI has been found to build up more slowly and diminish faster than trust in human beings (Hafızoğlu & Sen, 2018; Schmidt et al., 2020). However, trust is a dynamic concept that can have long-term effects on user behaviors such as repurchase/reuse intentions, commitment, and loyalty (Ameen et al., 2021; Bilgihan, 2016; Bitkina et al., 2020; Cheng & Jiang, 2021; Elbeltagi & Agag, 2016; Kim & Perterson, 2017 Rheu et al., 2021). In practice, information technologies have been widely used to manage the total worth of a customer’s business throughout the relationship period (i.e. customer lifetime value) (Bilgihan, 2016). Therefore, although users’ initial trust can be significant for the early promotional stage of AI applications, more longitudinal studies are required to determine the effect of user trust in AI on the customer lifetime value.
From the commercial point of view, organizations attach importance to user trust in AI as it contributes to the sales of related products and services. Thus, the purchase and repurchase intention was a focus of the recent studies on the outcomes of user trust in AI (Rai, 2020; Yen & Chiang, 2021). However, the value of a customer for an organization should be based not only on the purchase-related aspects but also on other engagements such as providing new product ideas or referral of new customers (Barari et al., 2021). Thus, customer/user engagement is a significant concept in evaluating consumers of products and

services from both transactional and non-transactional perspectives. Nevertheless, there is still a lack of research on this topic in the context of human-AI trust. Therefore, customer/user engagement is another important topic for future research on user trust in AI, considering its relation to trust and its consequences, such as commitment (Barari et al., 2021). Furthermore, some researchers, such as Mostafa & Kasamani (2021), have discussed customer engagement as a one-dimensional concept and identified it as one of the outcomes of initial trust in AI applications. However, customer/user engagement is a multifaceted concept that, similar to trust, includes cognitive, affective, and behavioral aspects (Barari et al., 2021). This, therefore, suggests the need for more comprehensive studies that explore and compare the interactions between AI users’ trust and engagement from the above-mentioned multiple perspectives.
Although our literature review identifies perceived risk as an antecedent of user trust in AI, some studies have reported a reversed relationship between these two constructs, specifically that the attenuated perceived risk is one of the outcomes of building user trust (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Gillath et al., 2021; Mou et al., 2017; Sutrop, 2019). This has led to confusion about the actual relationship between user trust and perceived risk. However, a few studies suggested perceived risk as an outcome of user trust that presents inconsistent model fits for their proposed theoretical frameworks (Mou et al., 2017). Notably, these studies tend to consider perceived risk and uncertainty as the same concept (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021); however, these two constructs should be distinguished (Yang & Wibowo, 2022). People perceive risks when they are able to clearly identify and evaluate the outcomes and probability of an unfavorable event. In contrast, perceived uncertainties indicate the ambiguity of future events, with people being unable to make precise predictions (Yang & Wibowo, 2020). As discussed, users’ trust is based on their confidence in the performance, process, and purpose of AI applications. This confidence should be based on the users’ ability to identify and evaluate risks (i.e. perceived risk as an antecedent of trust). Further, consumers tend to rely on their trust in the service provider to reduce the uncertainty in decision making when lacking other precise references (i.e. perceived uncertainty as the outcome of trust) (Yang et al., 2019). Therefore, it is necessary to distinguish the roles of perceived risk and uncertainty in future research on user trust in AI.
The current study explores the various factors affecting users’ trust in AI. These factors suggest that the influencing factors in trust building can be studied at the macro (e.g. national culture) and micro (e.g. users’ personality) levels. This approach is consistent with previous research on user trust in other relevant fields, e.g. e-business (Kim & Tadisina, 2007) and public services (Wilkes, 2014). Further, we proposed users’ trust in AI as a concept that includes multiple components. However, the existing literature on the various influencing factors tends to

