consider users’ trust as a one-dimensional construct. For example, previous studies on AI have demonstrated that users’ trust can be affected by factors related to the technology (Nasirian et al., 2017; Pitardi & Marriott, 2021; Qin et al., 2020), organization (Nordheim et al., 2019), context (Bilgihan 2016; Qin et al., 2020), culture (Robinson, 2020), and individual differences (Qin et al., 2020; Sheridan, 2019); nevertheless, these studies only discussed “trust” as a generic concept. Accordingly, this study suggested that an influencing factor may affect the multiple components of users’ trust in AI. For instance, individual differences are related to users’ generic trust (Sheridan, 2019). They also affect users’ cognitive, affective and behavioral trust (Khaksari & Keyvanpour, 2019), and the trust in the technology, organization (Łapińska et al., 2021) and specific applications (Oksanen et al., 2020; Qin et al., 2020). However, it still requires future studies to investigate whether other influencing factors can consistently affect the various components of users’ trust in AI.
Further, the comparison among different components of trust that can affect users’ perceptions and behaviors toward AI is also under-researched. For instance, Bedué and Fritzsche (2021) argue that trust can simultaneously affect users’ perceived benefits and intention to use AI. However, trust was only discussed as a generic concept reflected by ability, integrity and benevolence in their study. Although the study considered the applications of AI in various sectors, it did not clarify whether users’ trust in the organization and the technology similarly play similar or different roles in the change of perceptions and behaviors. By comparison, Li et al. (2021) distinguished the trust in the organization and the technology. Nevertheless, the study did not explore the outcome of building users’ trust based on the two distinctive aspects. Although existing research in this field tends to agree on users’ cognitive, affective, and behavioral changes as the outcome of trust building (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021; Fan et al., 2020; Kim et al., 2021; Meyer-Waarden & Cloarec, 2022; Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021; Moussawi et al., 2021; Park, 2020; Priyadarshinee et al., 2017; Rahman et al., 2021; Starke et al., 2021; Vasiljeva et al., 2021; Vourgidis et al., 2018), future studies should further test and compare the roles of various components of users trust. Furthermore, from the users’ decision-making perspective, how people trust and accept AI depends on their involvement with AI applications (Mostafa & Kasamani, 2021). In other words, people will use simpler decision rules when they believe some AI applications are less valuable or important (Bauer et al., 2006; Hansen, 2005). Thus, more empirical studies should be conducted to investigate whether all the components of trust are required for people’s acceptance of AI applications with different user involvement levels.
Last, this study was conducted from the user perspective. However, the interaction among users, organizations and society should not be ignored. Building users’ trust can contribute to the digitalization of organizations in different ways, including encouraging new product developments, and reducing

transaction costs and investment risks (Jelovac et al., 2021; Weber-Lewerenz, 2021). Therefore, it suggests more empirical studies to investigate the long-term impact of user trust on AI-powered organizations. Further, the “black-box” element of AI is still one of the important issues in this field because “the machine learning systems used for predicting an outcome remain opaque or cannot be adequately observed” (Elliott et al., 2021, p.180). Accordingly, the adoption of AI may cause unanticipated or unintended consequences that can either positively or negatively impact society in the long term (Pringle et al., 2016). For example, a higher level of user trust may be associated with a higher possibility of misuse of AI. Thus, further research in this area is also necessary.
Conclusion
The findings of this review provide insights into the field of user trust in AI. The review provides a detailed research outlook of the given area, including the components, influencing factors, and outcomes of user trust in AI. Based on the findings, a new comprehensive and conceptual framework was proposed for a better understanding of user trust in AI. From a theoretical perspective, the proposed framework builds on existing theories and provides potential future research avenues. Building on existing theoretical models (e.g. UTAUT and CDR), this framework presents a logical sequence linking the influences, components and outcomes, from a new perspective that is more related to AI trust-building. Thus, this study more extensively explores the key factors in relation to user trust in AI, and suggests the interactions among relevant constructs. However, the framework needs to be tested and validated in more specific contexts (e.g. collaborative filtering, content-based filtering, intelligent systems). From a practical perspective, this study helps AI-supported service providers understand the concept of user trust from various perspectives, and emphasizes the need to build trust based on different facets (i.e. technological, organizational, contextual, social, and user-related) to facilitate positive cognitive, affective, and behavioral changes among the users. Therefore, this review provides a foundation for future researchers and practitioners to obtain more comprehensive findings.
